  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Boletus miniato-olivaceus is a medium-sized bolete featuring a distinctive reddish-brown to olive-brown cap with bright red pores that age to orange-yellow. This mushroom is commonly found throughout New England and is generally considered inedible due to its bitter taste.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pore Color",
      "description": "Bright red to orange-red pores that gradually fade to yellow or olive-yellow with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Coloration",
      "description": "Reddish-brown to olive-brown cap surface, often with a mottled or uneven appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Yellowish stem with red spotting or reticulation, especially near the apex",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, typically appearing in summer and fall on well-drained soil.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its striking red pores that might suggest toxicity, Boletus miniato-olivaceus is not poisonous but is avoided by foragers due to its persistently bitter flavor that remains even after cooking.",
